<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>45-45-90 Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Find the hypotenuse, the missing leg, the area, and the perimeter of a 45-45-90 triangle from one known side.</p>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle is a right triangle whose three angles measure 45°, 45°, and 90°. It is called an isosceles right triangle because its two legs are equal in length, and it is called a special right triangle because its sides always follow the same fixed ratio, no matter how large or small the triangle is. The 45-45-90 triangle calculator on this page uses that ratio to find the hypotenuse, the missing leg, the area, and the perimeter from a single side length that you enter.</p>
  <p>In triangle ABC, angle A is 45°, angle B is 45°, and angle C is 90°. Side BC is the first leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, side AC is the second leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and side AB is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Because the two acute angles are equal, the two legs are equal, <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />, and the three sides always satisfy the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />, which means <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>If <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, then the second leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5]]')} /> cm, the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm, and the area is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{25}{2} = 12.5]]')} /> cm². The calculator can also work out the perimeter from the same leg, so one measurement is enough to describe the whole triangle. The sections below explain the ratio, the four calculator modes, and the formulas, with worked examples at every step.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate the Hypotenuse, Leg, Area, or Perimeter</h2>
  <p>Four modes cover every way to start from one known side.</p>
  <p>The calculator on this page has four modes, one for each measurement it can find. Pick the mode that matches the side you know and the measurement you need. Each mode asks for one input only, because the fixed ratio fills in everything else. The table below lists all four modes with the input each one needs and the result it returns.</p>
  <p>To use a mode, first work out which side you know. The side opposite a 45° angle is a leg, and either leg can serve as <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, because the legs are equal. The side opposite the 90° angle is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Enter that measurement as a positive number, pick a unit, and click the Calculate button. The result appears with step-by-step working, so you can see the formula and the substitution, not just the answer. Use Reset to clear the fields before starting a new problem.</p>
  <p>Different modes need different formulas because each one starts from a different known side. Finding the hypotenuse from a leg multiplies by √2, while finding a leg from the hypotenuse divides by √2. The area mode squares the leg and halves the result, and the perimeter mode adds all three sides. You never need to work out the missing sides yourself before using the area or perimeter modes, because the calculator finds them internally first.</p>
  <p>The four formulas are <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> for the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} /> for a leg, <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} /> for the area, and <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]')} /> for the perimeter. Keep the units the same through a calculation: a leg entered in centimeters gives a hypotenuse in centimeters and an area in square centimeters. The calculator does not find the circumradius, the inradius, or measurements for triangles that are not 45-45-90 triangles, so confirm the angles before you use it.</p>
