<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a 45-45-90 Triangle?</h2>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle is named for its three angles.</p>
  <p>It is a right triangle, which means one of its angles is exactly 90°. The other two angles are both 45°, and the name lists all three angles in order. Because the right angle is fixed and the other two angles are fixed as well, the shape of the triangle is fully determined: every 45-45-90 triangle has the same shape, and two such triangles differ only in size.</p>
  <p>It is called a special right triangle because its side lengths follow a fixed ratio. In most right triangles, knowing one side tells you nothing about the others. In a 45-45-90 triangle, knowing one side tells you all of them, because the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} /> locks the three sides together. That is why the calculator can find the hypotenuse, the area, and the perimeter from a single input.</p>
  <p>The three angles add up to 180°, as they must in every triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[45^\\circ + 45^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the longest side, because it sits opposite the largest angle, the 90° angle. The two legs are equal, because they sit opposite equal angles. The triangle can be as small as a few millimeters or as large as a building plot, and the angles stay the same while the sides scale up together. The ratio stays the same too, so a triangle with leg 5 cm and a triangle with leg 5 m are the same shape at different sizes.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-main.svg')} alt="Labeled 45-45-90 triangle ABC with angles 45, 45, and 90 degrees, equal legs a and b, and hypotenuse c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC: angles 45°, 45°, and 90°, with equal legs a and b and hypotenuse c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-isosceles -->
<section id="sec-isosceles" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is It Called an Isosceles Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The name has two parts, and each part describes one fact.</p>
  <p>Isosceles means a triangle with two equal sides. In a 45-45-90 triangle, the two equal sides are the legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Right triangle means a triangle with one 90° angle. Put the two words together and the name says exactly what the triangle is: a right triangle with two equal sides.</p>
  <p>The equal legs follow from the equal angles. In any triangle, equal angles sit opposite equal sides. Here the two acute angles are both 45°, so the sides opposite them must be equal. The side opposite angle A is <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, and the side opposite angle B is <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />. This is not a coincidence of one drawing: it holds for every 45-45-90 triangle, because the angles are always the same.</p>
  <p>The triangle is symmetric about the altitude drawn from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse. That altitude meets the hypotenuse at its midpoint and at a right angle, and it divides the large triangle into two smaller triangles that are congruent to each other. Each small triangle has angles 45°, 45°, and 90°, so each one is itself a 45-45-90 triangle, at half the scale. That symmetry is another way to see why the two legs must match: a fold along the altitude maps one leg onto the other.</p>
  <p>The altitude from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse also has a useful length: it equals half the hypotenuse. For a triangle with hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. This fact is handy when a problem asks for the height of the triangle measured from the hypotenuse, and it is one more consequence of the symmetry of the shape.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-legs -->
<section id="sec-legs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Two Equal Legs and Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse sits across from it.</p>
  <p>Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is one perpendicular side, side BC in triangle ABC. Leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the other perpendicular side, side AC. The two legs meet at vertex C, where they form the 90° angle, so they are perpendicular to each other. Either leg can serve as the base of the triangle, with the other leg acting as the height, which is why the area formula can use the two legs directly.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is side AB, opposite the 90° angle. It is the longest side of the triangle, because the largest angle always sits opposite the longest side. It is also the only side that is not a leg, so it must never be confused with either leg. The two legs are equal, but the hypotenuse is not equal to them: it is √2 times either leg, about 1.414 times as long.</p>
  <p>Because the legs are equal, either one can be used as the known leg in the calculator. If a problem gives leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> instead of leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, enter that value as the leg: the result is the same, since <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />. The same freedom does not apply to the hypotenuse, which needs its own mode. Identifying the hypotenuse is simple in any drawing: look for the small square marking the right angle, and the side across from it is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Opposite angle</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Length</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg a (BC)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg b (AC)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a, equal to leg a</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c (AB)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√2, longest side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
