</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-ratio -->
<section id="sec-ratio" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The 45-45-90 Triangle Side Ratio</h2>
  <p>The ratio 1:1:√2 fixes every 45-45-90 triangle.</p>
  <p>The side ratio of a 45-45-90 triangle is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a : b : c = 1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Each part of the ratio describes one side. The first leg represents one unit, the second leg also represents one unit, and the hypotenuse represents √2 units. Written with the leg as <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, the three sides are <span set:html={rich('[[a = a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = a]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. If the leg is 1 cm, the other leg is 1 cm and the hypotenuse is √2 cm. If the leg is 10 cm, the other leg is 10 cm and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>The ratio stays the same regardless of the size of the triangle, because all 45-45-90 triangles are similar. Scaling a triangle up or down multiplies every side by the same factor, so the proportions between the sides do not change. Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4]]')} /> cm as an example: the second leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} /> cm and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 5.657]]')} /> cm, and the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[4 : 4 : 5.657]]')} /> is the same as <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The value of √2 is approximately 1.41421356. It is an irrational number, so its decimal expansion never ends and never repeats, and no decimal equals it exactly. Keep the exact radical form whenever you can: writing <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm is exact, while 5.657 cm is a rounded approximation. Use the exact form in your working and in final answers when the question asks for an exact value, and give the decimal only when an approximate answer is needed.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-ratio.svg')} alt="Three bars comparing the two equal legs and the hypotenuse in the ratio 1 to 1 to square root of 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The fixed ratio 1:1:√2 compares the two equal legs with the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-why-sqrt2 -->
<section id="sec-why-sqrt2" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Square Root of 2 Appear?</h2>
  <p>The square root of 2 comes straight from the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
  <p>Start with the equal legs, <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />, and apply the Pythagorean theorem, which holds for every right triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the equal legs, replacing <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + a^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Combine the two identical terms:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[2a^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take the positive square root of both sides. Side lengths are positive, so the negative root is discarded:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>That is the whole reason √2 appears: two equal squares add to twice one square, and the square root of 2 is the number that undoes that doubling. This is a special relationship, not an approximate shortcut. It follows from the equal legs plus Pythagoras, and it holds for every 45-45-90 triangle without exception.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm as a numerical check:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = \\sqrt{5^2 + 5^2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = \\sqrt{50}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 5\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c \\approx 7.071 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The step from √50 to <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> works because <span set:html={rich('[[50 = 25 \\times 2]]')} />, and the square root of 25 is 5. Simplifying the radical in this way keeps the answer exact and makes the pattern visible: the hypotenuse is always the leg times √2, whatever the leg length is.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-pythagoras.svg')} alt="Pythagorean derivation showing a squared plus a squared equals c squared, leading to c equals a times square root of 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">From a² + a² = c², the hypotenuse must be a√2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-hypotenuse -->
<section id="sec-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Hypotenuse From One Leg</h2>
  <p>Multiply either leg by √2 to get the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse and <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is either leg. The formula is a direct reading of the side ratio: the hypotenuse represents √2 units for every 1 unit of leg, so the hypotenuse is √2 times the leg. Multiplication is required because the ratio is a scaling relationship, and √2 appears because Pythagoras forces the hypotenuse to be that much longer than a leg.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 5\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c \\approx 7.071 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is longer than either leg, as it must be: √2 is about 1.414, so multiplying by it increases the length. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm, and 7.071 cm is the decimal approximation. Keep the exact form when the question asks for it, and round the decimal only at the end of your working, never in the middle.</p>
  <p>Use this mode whenever a leg is the known side and the hypotenuse is the unknown one. Because the legs are equal, it does not matter which leg the problem gives: enter it as <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and the formula gives the same hypotenuse. A quick sense check helps: the hypotenuse should come out about 1.414 times the leg. If your answer is twice the leg, or only a little longer than the leg, you have used the wrong multiplier.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-hypotenuse.svg')} alt="45-45-90 triangle with leg 5 cm and hypotenuse labeled 5 times square root of 2, about 7.071 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Hypotenuse from a leg: a = 5 cm gives c = 5√2 ≈ 7.071 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-leg-hyp -->
<section id="sec-leg-hyp" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find a Leg From the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Divide the hypotenuse by √2 to get a leg.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is either leg and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. This formula comes from rearranging <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />: dividing both sides by √2 isolates <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Dividing by √2 reverses the original multiplication, so it undoes exactly what the forward formula did. The other leg has the same length, so one calculation gives both legs at once.</p>
  <p>The rationalized form is usually easier to work with:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{c\\sqrt{2}}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>It comes from multiplying the top and bottom of <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} /> by √2. The denominator becomes <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 2]]')} />, and the numerator becomes <span set:html={rich('[[c\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. The two forms give the same value, but the rationalized form has no square root in the denominator, which is the form exam answers expect.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 5\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a \\approx 7.071 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both legs are <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm, about 7.071 cm each. The leg is shorter than the hypotenuse, as it must be, since dividing by a number larger than 1 shrinks the value. If your answer comes out longer than the hypotenuse, you have multiplied instead of dividing, or divided by the wrong number.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-leg.svg')} alt="45-45-90 triangle with hypotenuse 10 cm and each leg labeled 5 times square root of 2, about 7.071 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Leg from hypotenuse: c = 10 cm gives a = b = 5√2 ≈ 7.071 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-area -->
<section id="sec-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a 45-45-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>The area needs only one leg, because the legs are equal.</p>
  <p>The area of any right triangle is half the product of its two legs, because the legs act as the base and the perpendicular height:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />, substitute to get the area from one leg alone:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Division by two is necessary because a triangle covers half the rectangle formed by its two legs. The final area is expressed in square units, such as square centimeters, because area measures surface, not length. A square with side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> has area <span set:html={rich('[[a^2]]')} />, and the triangle is exactly half of that square, which is another way to see why the formula is <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{6 \\times 6}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{36}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = 18 \\text{ cm}^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The calculator's area mode accepts the leg and calculates the area directly, so you enter only <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. If you know the hypotenuse instead of the leg, find the leg first with <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} />, then use <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} />. There is also a shortcut from the hypotenuse alone: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{c^2}{4}]]')} />, which gives the same result. For <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{100}{4} = 25]]')} /> cm².</p>
  <p>Area grows with the square of the leg, so doubling the leg quadruples the area. A triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 12]]')} /> cm has four times the area of the <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm triangle, 72 cm² instead of 18 cm², not twice it. That square relationship is easy to forget, so check it whenever a problem scales a triangle up or down.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-area-perimeter.svg')} alt="45-45-90 triangle with leg 6 cm, hypotenuse 6 times square root of 2 cm, area 18 square cm and perimeter about 20.485 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Area and perimeter from the leg: a = 6 cm gives A = 18 cm² and P ≈ 20.485 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-perimeter -->
<section id="sec-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter of a 45-45-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Add all three sides, then factor out the leg.</p>
  <p>The perimeter is the total length around the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a + b + c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, substitute those relationships:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 2a + a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factor out <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> to get the simplified form:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]]')} /></p>
  <p>All three side lengths are included, because the perimeter traces the full boundary of the triangle: down one leg, across the other leg, and back along the hypotenuse. Leaving out any side gives a shorter distance that is not the perimeter. Perimeter is a linear measurement, not an area measurement, so the answer is in plain units such as centimeters, never square centimeters.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 5(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 10 + 5\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P \\approx 17.071 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The exact form is <span set:html={rich('[[10 + 5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm, and 17.071 cm is the decimal form. The calculator's perimeter mode accepts the leg and adds the three sides internally. There is also a shortcut from the hypotenuse alone: <span set:html={rich('[[P = c(1 + \\sqrt{2})]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, that gives <span set:html={rich('[[P = 10(1 + \\sqrt{2}) \\approx 24.142]]')} /> cm. Perimeter grows in direct proportion to the leg, so doubling the leg doubles the perimeter.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-square -->
<section id="sec-square" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How a Square Diagonal Creates a 45-45-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>One diagonal cuts a square into two identical 45-45-90 triangles.</p>
  <p>A square has four equal sides and four 90° corners. Draw one diagonal across it, from one corner to the opposite corner, and the square splits into two triangles. Each triangle has two sides that are sides of the square, so those two sides are equal, and the angle between them is a corner of the square, 90°. The diagonal is the third side, opposite that right angle, so it is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The diagonal also bisects the two corner angles it passes through. Each corner of the square is 90°, and the diagonal splits it into two 45° angles. That gives each triangle angles of 45°, 45°, and 90°, which is exactly a 45-45-90 triangle. The two triangles are congruent: they have the same side lengths and the same angles, because they share the diagonal and each takes two sides of the same square.</p>
  <p>Use a square with side length <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. The diagonal <span set:html={rich('[[d]]')} /> is the hypotenuse of the triangle, so Pythagoras gives:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[d^2 = a^2 + a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[d^2 = 2a^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[d = a\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use a square with side 8 cm:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[d = 8\\sqrt{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[d \\approx 11.314 \\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>This is why square diagonal calculations always use the same √2 relationship. Whenever you need the diagonal of a square, multiply the side by √2; whenever you know the diagonal and need the side, divide it by √2. The same rule applies to square tiles, square picture frames, square plots of land, and any other square shape, because every square splits the same way.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('forty-five-ninety-square-diagonal.svg')} alt="Square of side 8 cm split by a diagonal into two congruent 45-45-90 triangles, diagonal about 11.314 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A square diagonal of side 8 cm is d = 8√2 ≈ 11.314 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Solving a 45-45-90 Triangle</h2>
  <p>One triangle, a = 5 cm, worked through from start to finish.</p>
  <p>Start with the leg <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm and find every other measurement of the triangle, one formula at a time. Each step uses a formula from the sections above, and the summary table at the end collects the results.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the second leg: the legs are equal, so <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5]]')} /> cm.</li>
    <li>Find the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm.</li>
    <li>Find the area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2} = \\frac{25}{2} = 12.5]]')} /> square centimeters.</li>
    <li>Find the perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a + b + c = 5 + 5 + 5\\sqrt{2} = 10 + 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 17.071]]')} /> cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>All four measurements describe the same triangle. The sides <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[7.071]]')} /> satisfy the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} /> and the Pythagorean theorem, <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 5^2 = 25 + 25 = 50 = (5\\sqrt{2})^2]]')} />. The area and the perimeter are two different kinds of measurement of that same triangle: the area covers its surface, while the perimeter traces its boundary. If you change the leg, every other value changes with it, but the ratio between the sides never changes.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Measurement</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Exact value</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Approximate value</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg a</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg b</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5√2 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">7.071 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Area A</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">25/2 cm²</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">12.5 cm²</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Perimeter P</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">10 + 5√2 cm</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">17.071 cm</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of 45-45-90 Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Eight worked examples, covering each calculator mode and a square diagonal.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Find the Hypotenuse From a Leg</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 4\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Exact answer: <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[c \\approx 5.657]]')} /> cm. The hypotenuse is about 1.414 times the leg, so 4 cm becomes about 5.657 cm. Keep the exact form <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm when the question asks for an exact value.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Find the Hypotenuse From a Larger Leg</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 8\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Exact answer: <span set:html={rich('[[8\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[c \\approx 11.314]]')} /> cm. This is the same calculation as Example 1 at twice the scale, and the answer is exactly twice as large, which confirms the linear scaling of side lengths. The same value is the diagonal of a square of side 8 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Find a Leg From the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} />. Rationalizing gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 8.485]]')} /> cm. Both legs are <span set:html={rich('[[6\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm, because the legs are equal. The leg is shorter than the hypotenuse, as it must be.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Find a Leg From a Larger Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c\\sqrt{2}}{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 14.142]]')} /> cm. Both legs are <span set:html={rich('[[10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. A quick check: <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{2} = 10\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 20]]')} /> cm, which returns the given hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Calculate the Area</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{49}{2}]]')} />. Result: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 24.5]]')} /> cm². The area is exact, with no square root and no rounding, because <span set:html={rich('[[7^2 = 49]]')} /> halves cleanly. Area is in square units, unlike the side lengths, so the unit is cm², not cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Calculate the Perimeter</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 6(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]')} />. Exact answer: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12 + 6\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[P \\approx 20.485]]')} /> cm. Adding the sides one by one gives the same result: <span set:html={rich('[[6 + 6 + 6\\sqrt{2} = 12 + 6\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Perimeter is in plain units, not square units.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Find a Square Diagonal</h3>
  <p>Given a square with side 10 m. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[d = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[d = 10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> m. Exact answer: <span set:html={rich('[[10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> m. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[d \\approx 14.142]]')} /> m. The diagonal divides the square into two 45-45-90 triangles, each with legs of 10 m and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> m. This is the same formula as finding a hypotenuse from a leg, because the diagonal is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Find the Hypotenuse From a Decimal Leg</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 2.5]]')} /> cm. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2.5\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Exact answer: <span set:html={rich('[[2.5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Decimal approximation: <span set:html={rich('[[c \\approx 3.536]]')} /> cm. Decimal inputs work exactly like whole-number inputs: multiply by √2, keep the exact form first, and round the decimal only at the final step. The second leg is also 2.5 cm.</p>
  <p>Exact answers and decimal approximations serve different purposes. The exact form, such as <span set:html={rich('[[4\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm, is precise and is what exam answers usually require. The decimal form, such as 5.657 cm, is easier to picture and to measure with a ruler. Give the exact form first, then the decimal, and round the decimal only at the final step.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-exact -->
<section id="sec-exact" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Exact Answers Versus Decimal Approximations</h2>
  <p>An exact answer keeps √2; a decimal answer rounds it.</p>
  <p>Compare the exact answer <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm with the decimal approximation 7.071 cm. The first is exact: it states the length precisely, using the symbol √2 to stand for the irrational number whose square is 2. The second is rounded: it states the length to three decimal places, which is close to the true value but not equal to it. The difference is small, about 0.00007 cm, but it is real, and it matters in proofs and in further calculations.</p>
  <p>The number √2 is irrational, which means it cannot be written as a fraction of two whole numbers, and its decimal expansion never terminates and never repeats. It begins 1.41421356 and continues without end. Any decimal you write for it, however long, is an approximation. That is why exact radical notation is preferable in mathematical proofs and in exam answers that ask for an exact value: the radical keeps the full precision that a decimal loses.</p>
  <p>A decimal approximation is more practical when you need to measure, cut, or build something. A ruler cannot measure <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm directly, but it can measure 7.071 cm. A carpenter cutting a diagonal brace needs the decimal, while a student proving a theorem needs the exact form. Use each form where it fits, and label decimals as approximate so nobody mistakes them for exact values.</p>
  <p>Rounding should be performed at the end of a calculation, not in the middle. If you replace √2 with 1.41 early and then multiply, every later step carries that error forward. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, using 1.41 gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.05]]')} /> cm, while the correct approximation is 7.071 cm. Keep √2 exact through the working, in the form <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, and round only the final answer. Never present a rounded decimal as if it were exact.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your 45-45-90 Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Four quick checks catch nearly every error.</p>
  <p>Run at least one check on every answer, and all four when the problem matters. The example below uses the triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm throughout.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Equal Legs</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />. Here both legs are 5 cm, so the check passes. If your two legs differ, one of them is wrong, or the triangle is not a 45-45-90 triangle at all. This is the fastest check of all, because it needs no arithmetic: the two legs must match exactly.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Side Ratio</h3>
  <p>Divide the hypotenuse by a leg: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{a} = \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{7.071}{5} \\approx 1.414]]')} />, so the ratio holds. If the division gives 2, you have used the 30-60-90 doubling by mistake. If it gives about 1.732, you have used √3 by mistake. The ratio <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{a}]]')} /> must be √2, and nothing else.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 5^2 = 25 + 25 = 50]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[(5\\sqrt{2})^2 = 25 \\times 2 = 50]]')} />, so the theorem holds. This check catches swapped legs and wrong multipliers, because using the wrong value for any side breaks the equality. The <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> runs this check for any three side lengths.</p>
  <h3>Check 4: Angle Sum</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ + 45^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ]]')} />. This check is about the problem setup rather than the arithmetic: if the angles of your triangle do not add to 180°, or if they are not 45°, 45°, and 90°, the 45-45-90 formulas do not apply at all, and no side length from them can be trusted. Confirm the angles first, then trust the sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of 45-45-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>Ten properties follow from the fixed ratio.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The triangle always has angles of 45°, 45°, and 90°, and those angles always add to 180°.</li>
    <li>Both legs are equal, <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />, because they sit opposite equal 45° angles.</li>
    <li>The hypotenuse is the longest side, because it sits opposite the 90° angle.</li>
    <li>The hypotenuse is √2 times either leg, <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.41421356]]')} />.</li>
    <li>All 45-45-90 triangles are similar, because they have the same three angles and the same side proportions.</li>
    <li>The side ratio <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} /> is independent of the size of the triangle, so scaling the triangle leaves the ratio unchanged.</li>
    <li>The altitude to the hypotenuse divides the triangle into two congruent smaller 45-45-90 triangles.</li>
    <li>The altitude to the hypotenuse equals half the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The area is half the square of a leg, <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} />, so doubling the leg quadruples the area.</li>
    <li>The perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2})]]')} />, so doubling the leg doubles the perimeter.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each property follows from the angles and the ratio, not from a single example. The equal angles force equal legs, the equal legs plus Pythagoras force the √2 hypotenuse, and the √2 hypotenuse forces the area and perimeter formulas. Once you accept the three angles, everything else is fixed, which is why one known side is enough to describe the whole triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-vs-3060 -->
<section id="sec-vs-3060" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>The two special triangles look alike, but their ratios never mix.</p>
  <p>Both are special right triangles, and both let you find every side from one known side. The difference is in the angles: a 45-45-90 triangle has two equal acute angles, while a 30-60-90 triangle has two different acute angles. That one difference changes the side ratio completely, so the formulas for one triangle give wrong answers for the other.</p>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle is isosceles, with two equal legs, while a 30-60-90 triangle is scalene, with no equal sides. In a 45-45-90 triangle the hypotenuse is √2 times a leg, while in a 30-60-90 triangle the hypotenuse is twice the short leg. The long leg of a 30-60-90 triangle is √3 times the short leg, and there is no matching side in a 45-45-90 triangle, because its two legs are the same length. For a full treatment of the other triangle, see the <a href="/30-60-90-triangle-calculator/">30-60-90 triangle calculator</a>, which uses the 1:√3:2 ratio.</p>
  <p>The formulas cannot be interchanged. Using <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2a]]')} /> on a 45-45-90 triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm gives 10 cm, while the correct hypotenuse is about 7.071 cm. Using <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> on a 30-60-90 triangle with short leg 5 cm gives about 7.071 cm, while the correct hypotenuse is 10 cm. Always confirm the angles before choosing a formula: equal acute angles mean 45-45-90, and angles of 30° and 60° mean 30-60-90.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45-45-90</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30-60-90</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Angles</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°, 45°, 90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30°, 60°, 90°</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side ratio</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:1:√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:√3:2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Equal sides</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Two equal legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No equal legs</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg × √2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Short leg × 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Long leg</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Same as short leg</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Short leg × √3</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Triangle type</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Isosceles right</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scalene right</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
