</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Solving 45-45-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>Fourteen errors show up again and again in 45-45-90 problems.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Assuming the hypotenuse equals twice the leg: doubling belongs to the 30-60-90 triangle. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, doubling gives 10 cm, while the correct hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Using √3 instead of √2: √3 belongs to the 30-60-90 triangle, whose ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} />. A 45-45-90 triangle always uses √2. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that both legs are equal: the two legs are always the same length, so if a problem gives one leg, the other leg is already known. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5]]')} /> cm, not a new unknown. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[a = b]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Treating the hypotenuse as one of the legs: the hypotenuse is the side opposite the 90° angle, and it is longer than either leg. Using it as a leg in the area formula gives a wrong area. Correct: identify the hypotenuse by the right-angle mark first.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{3}]]')} />: this mixes the two special triangles. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.660]]')} /> cm is neither the hypotenuse nor a leg of this triangle. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm.</li>
    <li>Dividing the hypotenuse by 2 instead of √2: halving gives the 30-60-90 short leg, not the 45-45-90 leg. For <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, halving gives 5 cm, while the correct leg is <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square the leg when calculating area: the area needs <span set:html={rich('[[a^2]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Using <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a}{2}]]')} /> for <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm gives 3 cm², while the correct area is <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{36}{2} = 18]]')} /> cm². Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Adding only the two legs when calculating perimeter: the perimeter needs all three sides. Adding just the two legs for <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm gives 10 cm, while the true perimeter is about 17.071 cm. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a + b + c]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Confusing exact answers with decimal approximations: <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm is exact, while 7.071 cm is rounded. Writing 7.071 cm as if it were exact loses the precision the radical keeps. Correct: give the exact form first, then the decimal.</li>
    <li>Rounding √2 too early: using 1.41 for √2 in the middle of a calculation shifts every later step. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[5 \\times 1.41 = 7.05]]')} /> cm instead of 7.071 cm. Correct: keep √2 exact through the working and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: a leg in centimeters and a hypotenuse in meters cannot be combined directly. Convert every measurement to one unit before calculating. Correct: 0.5 m equals 50 cm, then calculate in centimeters.</li>
    <li>Confusing the 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 ratios: the ratios <span set:html={rich('[[1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2]]')} /> belong to different triangles. Swapping them gives a hypotenuse that is wrong by a wide margin. Correct: confirm the angles, then pick the ratio that matches them.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that area requires square units: an area of 18 cm is wrong by unit, even if the number is right. Area measures surface, so it is 18 square centimeters. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 18]]')} /> cm².</li>
    <li>Using the formulas for an arbitrary right triangle: the formulas <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2}]]')} /> hold only when the angles are 45°, 45°, and 90°. A right triangle with angles 40° and 50° has a different ratio. Correct: confirm the angles first, then use the formulas.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of 45-45-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>The ratio turns up wherever a square is cut in half.</p>
  <p>The most common use is finding the diagonal of a square. A square tile with side 30 cm has a diagonal of <span set:html={rich('[[30\\sqrt{2} \\approx 42.426]]')} /> cm, which is the length to cut along when a tile must be split corner to corner. A square picture frame, a square table top, and a square plot of land all use the same calculation, because every square splits into two 45-45-90 triangles.</p>
  <p>Geometry homework and trigonometry exercises use 45-45-90 problems to test whether students can identify the equal legs, apply the ratio, and keep exact radical answers. A student who knows one side of such a triangle can find the rest without measuring, which is the point of the ratio. Classroom demonstrations use the triangle to show how a fixed ratio turns one measurement into a full description of a shape, and how cutting a square in half produces the triangle.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing, architectural layouts, and construction geometry use 45-45-90 relationships when a design calls for a 45° angle, such as a diagonal brace, a square-based pattern, or a corner cut at 45°. A builder who knows one side of such a triangle can find the diagonal distance without measuring it directly. Coordinate-plane work uses the same idea: a move of 5 units right and 5 units up covers a diagonal distance of <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071]]')} /> units. These are realistic uses of the geometry itself, not claims about engineering tolerances: real design work adds material limits and safety margins beyond the exact geometric values on this page.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Work all six problems on paper before reading the answers.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Find the Hypotenuse From a Leg</h3>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle has a leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm. Find the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Find a Leg From the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c = 14]]')} /> cm. Find the leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Calculate the Area</h3>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle has a leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 10]]')} /> cm. Find the area <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Calculate the Perimeter</h3>
  <p>A 45-45-90 triangle has a leg of <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm. Find the perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Find the Diagonal of a Square</h3>
  <p>A square has a side length of 12 cm. Find the length of its diagonal.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Verify a Proposed 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>A triangle has legs of 6 cm and 6 cm and a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[6\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm. Use the Pythagorean theorem to verify that these side lengths form a 45-45-90 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[c = a\\sqrt{2} = 9\\sqrt{2} \\approx 12.728]]')} /> cm. The exact answer is <span set:html={rich('[[9\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\frac{c\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{14\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 7\\sqrt{2} \\approx 9.899]]')} /> cm. Both legs are <span set:html={rich('[[7\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{a^2}{2} = \\frac{100}{2} = 50]]')} /> cm². The area is exact, with no rounding.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: <span set:html={rich('[[P = a(2 + \\sqrt{2}) = 3(2 + \\sqrt{2}) = 6 + 3\\sqrt{2} \\approx 10.243]]')} /> cm. Adding the sides one by one gives the same result: <span set:html={rich('[[3 + 3 + 3\\sqrt{2} = 6 + 3\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: <span set:html={rich('[[d = a\\sqrt{2} = 12\\sqrt{2} \\approx 16.971]]')} /> cm. The diagonal is the hypotenuse of the 45-45-90 triangle formed by cutting the square in half.</p>
  <p>Answer 6: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 36 + 36 = 72]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c^2 = (6\\sqrt{2})^2 = 36 \\times 2 = 72]]')} />. The two sides match, and the legs are equal, so the triangle is a 45-45-90 triangle with hypotenuse about 8.485 cm.</p>
</section>

